       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Amlodipine 30 mg is 3 times the usual maximum dose of 10 mg once daily. At that dose there is a real risk of severe hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and peripheral edema. Overdoses can also cause prolonged shock and non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema. Your patient's normal kidney and liver function and lack of allergies don't change that limit.\n\nCould you tell me which of these applies?\n\n- **The dose was a typo.** If you meant 5 mg or 10 mg once daily, I'll enter it right away. The usual start is 5 mg, or 2.5 mg for frail patients, and the maximum is 10 mg.\n- **You want more blood pressure control than 10 mg gives.** The usual next step is to add a second agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, an ARB, or a thiazide, rather than raise the amlodipine dose.\n- **You intend 30 mg on purpose.** Tell me the clinical rationale and I'll add it to the order note. I'd also suggest confirming with pharmacy first."
      }
